<title>USA: U.S. April durable goods orders rose 1.4 pct.</title>
<headline>U.S. April durable goods orders rose 1.4 pct.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1997-05-28</dateline>
<text>
<p>U.S. Commerce Department seasonally adjusted data on durable goods orders, with percent changes from prior months.</p>
<p>    PERCENT CHANGES:	Apr     Mar--Rev from     Feb</p>
<p>  New Orders		  1.4    -2.2    -2.6	 1.0</p>
<p>  Transportation Equip.   4.5    -3.9    -4.6	-7.2</p>
<p>  NonTransport.Equip.     0.5    -1.7    -2.6	 3.6</p>
<p>  NonDefense Orders	 0.6    -1.9    -2.3	 1.4</p>
<p>  Defense Cap Orders    +28.0   -12.2   -13.7     -11.1</p>
<p>  NonDefense Cap Orders  -1.3    -2.9    -4.0	 2.4</p>
<p>  -- Ex-Aircraft/Parts   -0.6    -0.9    -1.9	 7.2</p>
<p>    BILLIONS OF DLRS:    Apr	Mar---Rev from     Feb</p>
<p> New Orders		175.56   173.19   172.43    177.08</p>
<p> Transportation Equip.  39.36    37.68    37.41     39.21</p>
<p> NonTransport.Equip.   136.19   135.51   133.96    137.86</p>
<p> NonDefense Orders     169.36   168.35   167.67    171.56</p>
<p> Defense Cap Orders	6.19     4.84     4.76	5.51</p>
<p> NonDefense Cap Orders  44.95    45.53    45.02     46.91</p>
<p> -- Ex-Aircraft/Parts   40.03    40.25    39.82     40.61</p>
<p>    PERCENT CHANGES:	Apr     Mar--Rev from     Feb</p>
<p> Total Shipments	    1.1     0.9     0.6	 2.0</p>
<p> Total Unfilled Orders   -0.4    -0.4    -0.5	 0.7</p>
<p>    BILLIONS OF DLRS:    Apr	Mar---Rev from     Feb</p>
<p> Total Shipments	 177.30   175.36   174.86    173.74</p>
<p> Ttl Unfilled Orders   493.38   495.13   494.87    497.31</p>
<p> NEW ORDERS-PCT CHANGES   Apr     Mar--Rev from     Feb</p>
<p>  Primary Metals	    2.8     1.2     0.3	-0.5</p>
<p>  Industrial Machinery    2.2     0.2    -1.0	 2.0</p>
<p>  Electrical Machinery   -5.3    -6.0    -4.9	 7.8</p>
<p>NEW ORDERS-BILLIONS OF DLRS:</p>
<p>  Primary Metals	  16.45    16.01   15.86     15.82</p>
<p>  Industrial Machinery  33.94    33.22   32.82     33.15</p>
<p>  Electrical Machinery  27.26    28.78   29.13     30.63</p>
<p>Wall Street economists had expected April durable goods orders to rise 1.3 percent. Excluding defense orders, they expected a 0.8 percent increase.</p>
</text>
